## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates ability to translate privacy and safety requirements into a concrete, low-overhead component that precedes LLM calls and manages per-tenant policy enforcement.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy surface and versioning
- Lightweight, rule-based prompt classification
- Redaction of PII and disallowed content
- Caching decisions to minimize latency and repeated processing

## Code Example
```javascript
function classifyPrompt(prompt) {
  const p = prompt.toLowerCase();
  if (/ssn|credit card|password/.test(p)) return 'PII';
  if (p.includes('invoice') || p.includes('order')) return 'TRANSACTION';
  return 'GENERAL';
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a cross-tenant tool governance layer for an LLM-ops gateway with external integrations. It probes policy modeling, isolation, and auditability under realistic latency constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy enforcement
- Tool-usage planning and data-scope
- Sandboxed tool execution and per-tenant credentials
- Tamper-evident, signed audit logs
- Latency targets and test plans

## Code Example

```javascript
function evaluatePolicy(prompt, tenantId, toolList) {
  const policy = fetchPolicyForTenant(tenantId);
  // allowlist check
  const allowed = toolList.filter(t => policy.allowedTools.includes(t));
  // data scope check
  const dataOk = policy.dataScope.includes(extractDataScope(prompt));
  // rate limiting
  enforceRateLimit(tenantId, prompt);
  return { allowed, dataOk };
}
```

